Yeah.

1306
00:53:10,580 --> 00:53:15,580
But, but I mean, still humanoid robotics is also very difficult.

1307
00:53:15,580 --> 00:53:17,580
And so let's get back to that again.

1308
00:53:17,580 --> 00:53:28,580
And so one of your goals, I would say, is really to create humanoid robots, which can solve like repetitive or dangerous tasks for humans.

1309
00:53:28,580 --> 00:53:29,580
Yeah.

1310
00:53:29,580 --> 00:53:35,580
And what kind of applications do you actually foresee for humanoid robots?

1311
00:53:35,580 --> 00:53:38,580
So there are two ways of thinking about this.

1312
00:53:38,580 --> 00:53:39,580
Okay.

1313
00:53:39,580 --> 00:53:51,580
So for most of the application we see, right, let's say you are in a factory and you want to just move around in the factory that's pretty much flat and you want to do some tasks.

1314
00:53:51,580 --> 00:53:53,580
You don't need legs pretty much.

1315
00:53:53,580 --> 00:53:58,580
But if you think about a specific task, most of the time you can say, I don't need legs.

1316
00:53:58,580 --> 00:54:01,580
I can find a custom robot that can do this task.

1317
00:54:01,580 --> 00:54:06,580
But for me, it's like, if I understand how to do humanoid, pretty much I have solved all the tasks.

1318
00:54:06,580 --> 00:54:07,580
Right.

1319
00:54:07,580 --> 00:54:09,580
So this is the way I think about it.

1320
00:54:09,580 --> 00:54:12,580
But in terms of application, it's still construction.

1321
00:54:12,580 --> 00:54:19,580
Either we need to change the whole idea of construction, like, you know, 3D printing, building or something.

1322
00:54:19,580 --> 00:54:24,580
But if you are doing the same thing, we need humanoid for that, to automate the procedure.

1323
00:54:24,580 --> 00:54:26,580
Or if you need, if you press a button, you can do it.

1324
00:54:26,580 --> 00:54:27,580
Yeah.

1325
00:54:27,580 --> 00:54:33,580
And if you press fire fighting, it's very dangerous for human, disaster response, all this kind of thing.

1326
00:54:33,580 --> 00:54:46,580
But in, also for instance, if you want to have a service robot at home, most probably it should be human, you know, humanoid to do all the, you know, all the tasks that we do at home.

1327
00:54:46,580 --> 00:54:49,580
Yeah.

1328
00:54:49,580 --> 00:54:51,580
It depends, again.

1329
00:54:51,580 --> 00:54:53,580
Yeah, I definitely agree.

1330
00:54:53,580 --> 00:54:56,580
So, I mean, there's a very good case which you can make for humanoid robots.

1331
00:54:56,580 --> 00:55:08,580
I mean, that's, you know, evolution spent millions of years to come up with an optimal design and they came up with like humans for daily tasks.

1332
00:55:08,580 --> 00:55:20,580
But you could also make a case for the contrary, namely that for some very specific tasks, for example, vacuum cleaning.

1333
00:55:20,580 --> 00:55:22,580
Exactly.

1334
00:55:22,580 --> 00:55:25,580
Or just transporting people from A to B.

1335
00:55:25,580 --> 00:55:26,580
Yeah.

1336
00:55:26,580 --> 00:55:27,580
Or just flying people from A to B.

1337
00:55:27,580 --> 00:55:31,580
We have come up with very specific solutions to those problems.

1338
00:55:31,580 --> 00:55:37,580
And it seems that they are much better than the design we came up with, evolution.

1339
00:55:37,580 --> 00:55:49,580
So, is there still like a case which we can make for using humanoid robots or whenever we have a specific problem, maybe it's better to actually have like a specific hardware, specific.

1340
00:55:49,580 --> 00:55:52,580
So, again, I'm not looking at the base.

1341
00:55:52,580 --> 00:55:55,580
I don't believe that humanoid, human is the optimal morphology.

1342
00:55:55,580 --> 00:55:58,580
And I don't think about it like this.

1343
00:55:58,580 --> 00:56:03,580
But the way I think it is that to automate things in the world, you have two ways.

1344
00:56:03,580 --> 00:56:10,580
Either to change the world or you have built a robot that can work in this world.

1345
00:56:10,580 --> 00:56:14,580
So, the world around us, the human changed the whole shape.

1346
00:56:14,580 --> 00:56:16,580
And we optimize everything for our shape.

1347
00:56:16,580 --> 00:56:22,580
So, now if you have a robot that has the same shape, most probably they can do everything that humans want, right?

1348
00:56:22,580 --> 00:56:23,580
Yeah.

1349
00:56:23,580 --> 00:56:24,580
This is the way I think about it.

1350
00:56:24,580 --> 00:56:26,580
Otherwise, I don't think.

1351
00:56:26,580 --> 00:56:36,580
Again, it means that basically I'm looking at the applications that the robot can do all the things that humans can do for the human environment.

1352
00:56:36,580 --> 00:56:48,580
And for that, then it's difficult to think about any other, you know, morphology or modality of, you know, motion or interaction that could work better.

1353
00:56:48,580 --> 00:56:49,580
Yeah.

1354
00:56:49,580 --> 00:56:52,580
That makes actually a lot of sense.

1355
00:56:52,580 --> 00:56:53,580
Let's give the example of this.

1356
00:56:53,580 --> 00:56:56,580
Let's give the example of, you know, so we have the trains.

1357
00:56:56,580 --> 00:56:57,580
And trains are working.

1358
00:56:57,580 --> 00:57:03,580
So, we had to make a lot of, you know, rails and so on so that the train can work.

1359
00:57:03,580 --> 00:57:07,580
And also, we built our cities and so on with this.

1360
00:57:07,580 --> 00:57:13,580
Not that, but I mean, we already built a huge infrastructure of the streets and so on, right?

1361
00:57:13,580 --> 00:57:19,580
Now, what you can do is that you can make your city compatible with autonomous driving.

1362
00:57:19,580 --> 00:57:22,580
Or you need to solve the autonomous driving problem in the city.

1363
00:57:22,580 --> 00:57:23,580
So, which one is better?

1364
00:57:23,580 --> 00:57:24,580
I don't know.

1365
00:57:24,580 --> 00:57:32,580
But most probably, we need to adapt the technology to the environment we have rather than changing the whole environment for the robots.

1366
00:57:32,580 --> 00:57:33,580
Makes sense.

1367
00:57:33,580 --> 00:57:34,580
Yeah.

1368
00:57:34,580 --> 00:57:42,580
And so, one big problem which roboticists face is this Moravec's paradox.

1369
00:57:42,580 --> 00:57:43,580
Yeah.

1370
00:57:43,580 --> 00:57:51,580
Which basically says that something which seems to be very easy for a human, it's very difficult for robots to do and it's the other way around.

1371
00:57:51,580 --> 00:57:52,580
Yeah.

1372
00:57:52,580 --> 00:57:59,580
So, why do we actually think that this is the case and what can we do about it?

1373
00:57:59,580 --> 00:58:01,580
I don't know why this is the case.

1374
00:58:01,580 --> 00:58:08,580
Maybe it's a very, very long time of evolution that gives us this physical capability.

1375
00:58:08,580 --> 00:58:11,580
And the cognitive part is very new, right?

1376
00:58:11,580 --> 00:58:12,580
It's not something that...

1377
00:58:12,580 --> 00:58:15,580
So, I think that has been much more difficult.

1378
00:58:15,580 --> 00:58:21,580
The fact that it becomes very unconscious makes us feel that it's a very difficult, very easy thing.

1379
00:58:21,580 --> 00:58:26,580
While it's not easy because it took a long, long time for us to develop.

1380
00:58:26,580 --> 00:58:40,580
A lot of species, you know, gradually started to go from, you know, in the sea to the, you know, on the land and then just crawling on the land and first and then going on two legs and so on, right?

1381
00:58:40,580 --> 00:58:42,580
It took a long time.

1382
00:58:42,580 --> 00:58:48,580
But now the cognitive process that we are talking about, like, you know, playing chess or so on, this is a new thing, right?

1383
00:58:48,580 --> 00:58:50,580
And since it's more conscious...

1384
00:58:50,580 --> 00:58:52,580
And for humans, it's very difficult.

1385
00:58:52,580 --> 00:58:57,580
We think that it's very difficult for computers, but computers work differently.

1386
00:58:57,580 --> 00:59:00,580
They can do a lot of computation, right?

1387
00:59:00,580 --> 00:59:04,580
And they don't need to interact with the world, physical world.

1388
00:59:04,580 --> 00:59:07,580
And I think that's the main reason.

1389
00:59:07,580 --> 00:59:17,580
So, the thing that are difficult for us, it's really easy for computers because they can do a lot of computation in parallel and then, you know, find brute force the solution.

1390
00:59:17,580 --> 00:59:19,580
You cannot brute force solution in your head.

1391
00:59:19,580 --> 00:59:20,580
Right.

1392
00:59:20,580 --> 00:59:22,580
And that's the real problem in the real world.

1393
00:59:22,580 --> 00:59:24,580
You need to do the job.

1394
00:59:24,580 --> 00:59:33,580
You need to just, you know, you know how many people or how many species died until now we can not walk, you know?

1395
00:59:33,580 --> 00:59:39,580
Do you think we should actually follow the path of evolution to come up with those systems?

1396
00:59:39,580 --> 00:59:49,580
Or do you think it's better that we engineered ourselves in the same way as we engineered flying, driving, vacuum cleaning even?

1397
00:59:49,580 --> 00:59:50,580
We cannot afford that, right?

1398
00:59:50,580 --> 00:59:51,580
So, it's clear.

1399
00:59:51,580 --> 00:59:53,580
We cannot go the path of evolution.

1400
00:59:53,580 --> 00:59:54,580
It's very costly.

1401
00:59:54,580 --> 00:59:55,580
It's very time consuming.

1402
00:59:55,580 --> 01:00:01,580
So, you don't think that, for example, evolutionary robotics could be a good path forward?

1403
01:00:01,580 --> 01:00:04,580
You cannot do evolutionary robotics in the real world, right?

1404
01:00:04,580 --> 01:00:12,580
You can think about doing in simulation, which is, again, it's an abstraction, which means that you are using human knowledge to build physics simulation.

1405
01:00:12,580 --> 01:00:14,580
So, this is already a shortcut.

1406
01:00:14,580 --> 01:00:16,580
You are not going to a path of evolution.

1407
01:00:16,580 --> 01:00:17,580
You can get inspiration.

1408
01:00:17,580 --> 01:00:18,580
Inspiration.

1409
01:00:18,580 --> 01:00:20,580
And do evolution strategy.

1410
01:00:20,580 --> 01:00:33,580
But it's not that you are going the path of evolution, which means that you saw that there are, I don't know, whatever in the real world for millennia or I don't know for how many years, such that you do what.

1411
01:00:33,580 --> 01:00:35,580
We cannot afford to afford that.

1412
01:00:35,580 --> 01:00:38,580
So, for sure, we need to engineer this.

1413
01:00:38,580 --> 01:00:47,580
But since it's very, you know, just normal for human, we shouldn't be deceived that it's an easy thing.

1414
01:00:47,580 --> 01:00:50,580
It's a super difficult thing, right?

1415
01:00:50,580 --> 01:01:01,580
So, humans should be more appreciative of the things which we saw every day in the way of motion.

1416
01:01:01,580 --> 01:01:09,580
You know, our hands, our hands is the most complex, most difficult thing ever, you know, to me.

1417
01:01:09,580 --> 01:01:14,580
So, the body, the part of the body always, you know, impresses me is the hand.

1418
01:01:14,580 --> 01:01:15,580
It's crazy.

1419
01:01:15,580 --> 01:01:16,580
And we are far from having that.

1420
01:01:16,580 --> 01:01:20,580
We are far from having something that is even 1% of this hand.

1421
01:01:20,580 --> 01:01:23,580
That's a good change of topic.

1422
01:01:23,580 --> 01:01:36,580
So, what would you say are actually the main bottlenecks to move humanoid robots around in the same way so that they are also as useful as humans?

1423
01:01:36,580 --> 01:01:40,580
What would you say are really the big things which we have to solve?

1424
01:01:40,580 --> 01:01:42,580
I think pretty much everything.

1425
01:01:42,580 --> 01:01:43,580
Okay.

1426
01:01:43,580 --> 01:01:45,580
So, pretty much we haven't solved anything, you know.

1427
01:01:45,580 --> 01:01:46,580
We just have to.

1428
01:01:46,580 --> 01:01:48,580
We just did very, very few steps.

1429
01:01:48,580 --> 01:01:51,580
So, still, let's say, let's go back.

1430
01:01:51,580 --> 01:01:53,580
Let's not talk about human.

1431
01:01:53,580 --> 01:01:58,580
Let's talk about can you have a quadruped robot that gets the intelligence of dog?

1432
01:01:58,580 --> 01:02:01,580
Still, you are not at all there, right?

1433
01:02:01,580 --> 01:02:03,580
Or let's talk about the baby.

1434
01:02:03,580 --> 01:02:05,580
Do we have that intelligence?

1435
01:02:05,580 --> 01:02:06,580
Not at all.

1436
01:02:06,580 --> 01:02:09,580
So, let's not talk about the grown-up human, right?

1437
01:02:09,580 --> 01:02:15,580
And I think that the last, you know, just this fancy thing like playing with the human.

1438
01:02:15,580 --> 01:02:18,580
Or anything like playing chess or I don't know.

1439
01:02:18,580 --> 01:02:28,580
This kind of things are, it seems very difficult, but these are very early steps toward understanding how the movement in the real world works.

1440
01:02:28,580 --> 01:02:29,580
And still, they are related.

1441
01:02:29,580 --> 01:02:31,580
A lot of them are decision-making.

1442
01:02:31,580 --> 01:02:37,580
But still, they are really baby steps toward really going to human.

1443
01:02:37,580 --> 01:02:41,580
So, I think we should take few steps back, right?

1444
01:02:41,580 --> 01:02:44,580
Still, if you have something that is as intelligent as human.

1445
01:02:44,580 --> 01:02:46,580
As intelligent as a dog or a cat.

1446
01:02:46,580 --> 01:02:48,580
We should be very happy.

1447
01:02:48,580 --> 01:02:49,580
Right?

1448
01:02:49,580 --> 01:02:51,580
And then the next step would be a baby.

1449
01:02:51,580 --> 01:02:55,580
And then maybe, I don't know when we can go to the same.

1450
01:02:55,580 --> 01:03:01,580
So, you would basically say the first step is to recreate a cat or a dog.

1451
01:03:01,580 --> 01:03:03,580
The amount of intelligence they have.

1452
01:03:03,580 --> 01:03:05,580
Not the morphology necessarily, right?

1453
01:03:05,580 --> 01:03:09,580
And so, what kind of issues do we face there?

1454
01:03:09,580 --> 01:03:13,580
And how would you basically divide the problem into smaller problems?

1455
01:03:13,580 --> 01:03:14,580
Yeah.

1456
01:03:14,580 --> 01:03:16,580
There are many things.

1457
01:03:16,580 --> 01:03:19,580
You know, I have, yeah.

1458
01:03:19,580 --> 01:03:27,580
So, I have some unpopular opinions that normally actually not everybody agrees with me.

1459
01:03:27,580 --> 01:03:28,580
Okay.

1460
01:03:28,580 --> 01:03:29,580
I like that already.

1461
01:03:29,580 --> 01:03:30,580
Go on.

1462
01:03:30,580 --> 01:03:35,580
I think, you know, the thing that people do nowadays, the focus of the research.

1463
01:03:35,580 --> 01:03:38,580
I do not think we are going in that direction.

1464
01:03:38,580 --> 01:03:42,580
So, we are going in the direction that we need to have a lot of knowledge.

1465
01:03:42,580 --> 01:03:46,580
We need to have a lot of data to solve the problem.

1466
01:03:46,580 --> 01:03:48,580
I don't think this is the way to go.

1467
01:03:48,580 --> 01:03:53,580
But still, there are many smarter people than me that they think this is the way to go.

1468
01:03:53,580 --> 01:03:57,580
So, it doesn't mean that this, you know, that the community believes in it.

1469
01:03:57,580 --> 01:04:03,580
But I personally, with a few others that, you know, I talk to, I think it's not the way to go.

1470
01:04:03,580 --> 01:04:07,580
This is the only thing, the way that we know how to do.

1471
01:04:07,580 --> 01:04:10,580
There are difficult problems that we don't know how to solve.

1472
01:04:10,580 --> 01:04:11,580
And we pretend.

1473
01:04:12,580 --> 01:04:14,580
We pretend that they don't exist.

1474
01:04:14,580 --> 01:04:23,580
So, for instance, one of the things that we as human or most of the animal or a baby do is that we do continual learning.

1475
01:04:23,580 --> 01:04:26,580
Which means that we learn a small task, right?

1476
01:04:26,580 --> 01:04:32,580
And then when we learn a few tasks, it's not only for the sake of that task.

1477
01:04:32,580 --> 01:04:36,580
For finding some invariance in the world such that we can leverage.

1478
01:04:36,580 --> 01:04:40,580
And when we want to learn a new task, we can quickly learn that.

1479
01:04:40,580 --> 01:04:43,580
And this we don't have in the machine learning community, for instance.

1480
01:04:43,580 --> 01:04:45,580
What we do is that we learn a task.

1481
01:04:45,580 --> 01:04:48,580
For learning a new task, we start from scratch again.

1482
01:04:48,580 --> 01:04:50,580
And this is a very difficult problem.

1483
01:04:50,580 --> 01:04:52,580
That's why nobody is thinking about that, I think.

1484
01:04:52,580 --> 01:04:54,580
I mean, people are thinking about this.

1485
01:04:54,580 --> 01:04:58,580
But in robotics, no, very few people are going that path.

1486
01:04:58,580 --> 01:05:03,580
So, does it mean that you're not really a big believer in machine learning?

1487
01:05:03,580 --> 01:05:05,580
Of course, machine learning is important.

1488
01:05:05,580 --> 01:05:07,580
No, I am a believer.

1489
01:05:07,580 --> 01:05:09,580
But I think, you know, machine learning is very...

1490
01:05:10,580 --> 01:05:12,580
Fast, very big.

1491
01:05:12,580 --> 01:05:16,580
I think the problems that are super important, we are not spending enough time on.

1492
01:05:16,580 --> 01:05:18,580
So, one of the things is that...

1493
01:05:18,580 --> 01:05:25,580
So, this thing I discussed a lot yesterday and yesterday with people here in Berlin.

1494
01:05:25,580 --> 01:05:31,580
And so, one thing that we always deceive ourselves with is that we have a simulation.

1495
01:05:31,580 --> 01:05:34,580
And then we can say, we can go to the simulation.

1496
01:05:34,580 --> 01:05:37,580
We do not need to do the real world trial and error.

1497
01:05:37,580 --> 01:05:39,580
And we do trial and error there.

1498
01:05:39,580 --> 01:05:40,580
But...

1499
01:05:40,580 --> 01:05:44,580
To me, it's impossible to get a simulation like the real world, whatever we do.

1500
01:05:44,580 --> 01:05:45,580
Right?

1501
01:05:45,580 --> 01:05:46,580
It doesn't matter.

1502
01:05:46,580 --> 01:05:47,580
But there will be a gap.

1503
01:05:47,580 --> 01:05:53,580
So, at the end of the day, you need to at least fine-tune your behaviors in the real world.

1504
01:05:53,580 --> 01:05:54,580
Right?

1505
01:05:54,580 --> 01:06:00,580
Which means that if you just do the current existing type of learning behavior, reinforcement learning behavior,

1506
01:06:00,580 --> 01:06:05,580
all of them will just, you know, break the robot quickly if you want to improve it.

1507
01:06:05,580 --> 01:06:07,580
Which means that we are not going in that direction.

1508
01:06:07,580 --> 01:06:09,580
How to explore in the real world safely.

1509
01:06:10,580 --> 01:06:14,580
This is a fundamental question that will always exist.

1510
01:06:14,580 --> 01:06:17,580
And we never could, you know, answer it.

1511
01:06:17,580 --> 01:06:18,580
And we are not thinking about it.

1512
01:06:18,580 --> 01:06:19,580
Why?

1513
01:06:19,580 --> 01:06:22,580
Because we deceived ourselves that we can always go to simulation.

1514
01:06:22,580 --> 01:06:24,580
And in simulation, there is no safety problem.

1515
01:06:24,580 --> 01:06:28,580
The robot can fly around, go, you know, smash through the wall and nothing happens.

1516
01:06:28,580 --> 01:06:34,580
And so, what would you say is actually missing to have this kind of like continual learning in the real world?

1517
01:06:34,580 --> 01:06:35,580
There are many things.

1518
01:06:35,580 --> 01:06:37,580
So, first is the framework.

1519
01:06:37,580 --> 01:06:38,580
We don't know how to do it.

1520
01:06:38,580 --> 01:06:39,580
Mm-hmm.

1521
01:06:39,580 --> 01:06:40,580
The second is safety.

1522
01:06:40,580 --> 01:06:47,580
So, if you go to explore for a new task, it means that you need to make sure that you are not breaking yourself.

1523
01:06:47,580 --> 01:06:49,580
Because that's done, right?

1524
01:06:49,580 --> 01:06:50,580
Mm-hmm.

1525
01:06:50,580 --> 01:06:55,580
So, you shouldn't make catastrophic damage either to the environment or to yourself.

1526
01:06:55,580 --> 01:07:04,580
And still, theoretically, continual learning is not very well established, you know, framework given in machine learning.

1527
01:07:04,580 --> 01:07:05,580
Mm-hmm.

1528
01:07:05,580 --> 01:07:07,580
You let alone the robotic side.

1529
01:07:07,580 --> 01:07:10,580
And now, it's much more difficult when you go to the physical world.

1530
01:07:10,580 --> 01:07:11,580
Yeah.

